 NOTES = """\
-Sections 2.1--2.2 fix the native problem and the counting-constraint semantics:
-a plan maps steps to authorized users, and (1,S) requires a different user for
-every step in S. Section 6 and Theorem 6.5 explicitly build the authorization
-bipartite graph and use Hopcroft-Karp maximum matching to obtain a plan. This
-module hands the solver exactly those objects; no convenience reduction is used.
+Section 2 fixes the native objects and semantics: a plan maps every workflow step
+to an authorized user, and the counting constraint (1,S) assigns every participating
+user exactly one step.  Section 3, Theorem 3.6 says counting-constraint WSP is FPT.
+More decisively for this special case, Section 6, Theorem 6.5 constructs the
+authorization bipartite graph and invokes Hopcroft-Karp matching; because (1,S) is
+all-different, a full matching is already a complete plan.  That polynomial method
+rules out Track A and is reported as the successful Track B reference algorithm.
 
-STEP 0 rules out Track A. Theorem 3.4 supplies an FPT route for regular
-constraints, Proposition 6.2 covers (1,t,S)-style constraints, and Theorem 6.5's
-proof gives the polynomial matching algorithm used here. Its certificate is the
-output of that algorithm, so this is honestly Track B. Mechanical matching is
-reported as a successful reference algorithm; the tested difficulty is the gap
-between that route and recognizing one linear matching hidden in the labels.
+Generation is inverse and never solves its output.  It samples a nonzero slope a and
+a uniform set B of offsets in Z_p.  Every displayed edge has the form u=a*s+b for
+some b in B, so each offset produces a certified perfect matching and every edge is
+drawn from the same certificate-bearing construction.  One offset is sampled as the
+stored witness.  Row and display orders are shuffled independently.
 
-Generation is inverse. A multiplier and distinct step labels are sampled first;
-their products modulo q form a perfect matching and the planted plan. Independent
-random permutations add decoy authorized pairs. Every permutation contributes
-one edge at each step and user, and row order is shuffled, so planted and decoy
-edges have identical degree and position statistics. Attacks test row minima,
-row maxima, label-nearest choices, greedy unused-user assignment, and 256 random
-greedy restarts. Canonicalization sorts intrinsic step labels, user identifiers,
-and authorization sets; G8 tests step renumbering and every input-order symmetry.
+The compact route notices that sum(A_s)=|B|*a*s+sum(B) modulo p.  At the shipping
+preset p=251=10*25+1, so the inverse of 25 is -10; summing rows 0 and 1 reveals a,
+and any entry of row 0 supplies an offset.  Repeated modular addition writes a plan.
+The outlier attack is neutralized by exact regularity on both sides.  Row-minimum,
+greedy-unused, 256 random greedy restarts, and the obvious slope-one ansatz are all
+measured.  Canonicalization forgets opaque IDs and normalizes the recovered offset
+set under every affine change of cyclic coordinates.
 """
 
 
