-## Caveats
-
-This is deliberately Track B: any matching implementation makes the family easy, and the measured random-guess density does not imply computational hardness. It describes only a uniform prior over user permutations after enforcing the obvious all-different rule. The auxiliary linear labels are synthetic benchmark structure rather than a theorem of the paper. Row-minimum, row-maximum, label-nearest, single-pass greedy, 256-restart greedy, and full Hopcroft–Karp attacks were tested; LP/ILP and vendor-specific tool use were not, because exact matching already dominates them here. Most importantly, no claim about model difficulty is justified until the bare and hinted oracle runs complete.
