codex
Implemented a paper-native Track B generator, but it is not yet shippable because the OpenRouter quota is exhausted.

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1205.0852/gen_1205_0852.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1205.0852/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1205.0852/README.md)
- [Bare transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1205.0852/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1205.0852/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1205.0852/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The family uses native WSP plans with the paper’s `(1,S)` all-different counting constraint. It is correctly labeled Track B because Theorem 6.5 explicitly supplies Hopcroft–Karp matching; Theorem 3.6 also identifies counting-constraint WSP as FPT. [Full paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1205.0852)

Local results:

- G1–G9(c) pass.
- Guessing: 0/200,000 valid permutations.
- Five attacks: 0/8 each.
- Hopcroft–Karp: 8/8, 71,368 total edge scans.
- Compact route: 238 modular operations.
- Answer: 687 characters, 199 elements.
- Canonical-key invariance and distinctness: 20/20.

The prescribed harness was run for all three arms, but every revised call returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`. Those errors are recorded and were not misreported as model failures. After replenishing the OpenRouter limit, rerun the commands documented in the README; do not submit before obtaining a scored STEP 4 verdict.
